// expect-error: NITPICK-RUNG-001
// expect-no-parse-error
//
// `+` on two strings is CONCATENATION and is real Nitpick (TYPE_REFERENCE
// section 4). The seed does not lower it -- and the reason this test exists is
// what the seed did before it was made to say so.
//
// Every binary operator in subset 1 lowers to ONE LLVM instruction over the left
// operand's type, and `add`/`icmp` accept only `iN`. The checker was asking
// nothing about the operand type, so `"a" + "b"` sailed through and the emitter
// wrote `add { ptr, i64, i64 }`. That is invalid IR: llc rejected it one stage
// later, pointing at a line number in a generated file, with no way back to the
// source that meant it.
//
// The class matters more than the instance. A silently mis-lowered operator is
// the worst shape a compiler bug takes -- it is not that the wrong thing was
// rejected, it is that the wrong thing was ACCEPTED and something downstream had
// to notice. Subset 1's whole guarantee is that what the seed accepts, it lowers
// correctly. Anything outside it must be refused here, by name.
//
// Use `string_concat` instead, which the runtime floor provides.

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    string:a = "left";
    string:b = "right";
    string:joined = a + b;
    exit 0i32;
};

func:failsafe = int32(tbb32:_~err) { exit 1i32; };
